{"answer":"Django’s testing framework is built around Python’s standard unittest library but adds a large amount of Django‑specific support.  The core test classes live in the ``django.test`` package – ``TestCase`` and ``TransactionTestCase`` are imported by the test runner (see ``tests/runtests.py`` which imports ``django.test.TestCase`` and ``django.test.TransactionTestCase``).  ``TestCase`` creates a temporary test database and wraps each test method in a transaction that is rolled back, while ``TransactionTestCase`` allows tests to manage transactions manually.  For web‑application testing Django provides a lightweight HTTP client (``django.test.Client``) that can issue GET, POST, PUT, etc. requests against URL patterns without running a live server, enabling fast functional tests of views, middleware and templates.  When integration‑level testing is needed, ``LiveServerTestCase`` (and the Selenium‑based ``django.test.selenium`` classes) spin up a temporary live server that Selenium can drive.

Test discovery and execution are handled by ``django.test.runner``.  The runner (imported in ``tests/runtests.py`` as ``django.test.runner.get_max_test_processes`` and ``django.test.runner.parallel_type``) discovers tests in installed apps, can run them in parallel processes, and respects settings such as ``TEST_RUNNER``.  Utility helpers in ``django.test.utils`` (e.g., ``get_runner``, ``TimeKeeper``) provide the actual runner instance and timing information.

In summary, Django implements its web‑app testing framework by extending unittest with database management, a request‑simulation client, live‑server support, Selenium integration, and a configurable, parallel‑capable test runner that discovers tests across the project’s apps.","reference":"tests/runtests.py imports (lines showing django.test.TestCase, TransactionTestCase, django.test.runner, django.test.selenium, django.test.utils)"}

